WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.669
Strong validation starts with a clear contract.

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:07.072
Before processing data, define what the application expects: type, length, format, range,

00:00:07.072 --> 00:00:11.476
required fields, optional fields, allowed values, nested objects, arrays, and business rules.

00:00:11.476 --> 00:00:13.310
If the contract is vague,

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:17.714
validation usually becomes a collection of partial checks that are hard to

00:00:17.714 --> 00:00:19.916
test and easy to bypass accidentally.

00:00:19.916 --> 00:00:22.117
Type checks alone are not enough.

00:00:22.117 --> 00:00:26.154
A value may be a string but still be too long,

00:00:26.154 --> 00:00:29.823
in the wrong format, or unacceptable for the current workflow.

00:00:29.823 --> 00:00:33.493
A number may parse successfully but exceed an allowed range.

00:00:33.493 --> 00:00:37.897
An array may contain the right general type but include too many

00:00:37.897 --> 00:00:39.731
items or unexpected nested objects.

00:00:39.731 --> 00:00:45.603
A boolean may be valid syntactically but not a field the user is allowed to set.

00:00:45.603 --> 00:00:47.437
Business rules complete the contract.

00:00:47.437 --> 00:00:50.740
A start date should come before an end date.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:54.043
A role value should come from an approved set.

00:00:54.043 --> 00:00:58.079
An account identifier should match the authenticated user or tenant context.

00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:02.116
A price, discount, or workflow state should follow the application's rules.

00:01:02.116 --> 00:01:06.152
Well-formed data can still be unsafe when it violates business meaning.

00:01:06.152 --> 00:01:09.455
Contracts should be visible to developers, testers, and reviewers.

00:01:09.455 --> 00:01:12.023
Schema definitions, shared validation functions, API specifications,

00:01:12.023 --> 00:01:16.427
TypeScript interfaces paired with runtime schemas, and documented request models all help.

00:01:16.427 --> 00:01:18.262
The more explicit the contract,

00:01:18.262 --> 00:01:22.665
the easier it is to reject invalid input consistently and test the

00:01:22.665 --> 00:01:24.500
behavior when future changes arrive.
